WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.343
Hello and welcome, everyone! I'm&nbsp;James Murphy.

00:00:02.343 --> 00:00:03.510
And welcome to mCoding

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.780
where we try to get just a little bit&nbsp;
 better at programming every episode.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:12.960
Here's a trap that every single Python 
 programmer&nbsp;will fall for at some point in their career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.668
There's pretty much no setup.

00:00:14.668 --> 00:00:20.461
All I've got is a&nbsp;small class with a 
 class variable "__count," a getter, and a setter.

00:00:20.461 --> 00:00:25.140
We create an instance of&nbsp;
 the class and print the count just as a control.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.409
Then, instead of using our setter, we&nbsp;
 manually reach in and try to change the count.

00:00:29.409 --> 00:00:31.366
And then we print it.

00:00:31.366 --> 00:00:34.560
Couldn't get much&nbsp;
 simpler of an example than that, right?

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.800
So what do you think it'll 
 print&nbsp;when I run this main function?

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.237
I'd give you a minute to think about it.

00:00:39.237 --> 00:00:42.180
But it&nbsp;seems like the answer should be very obvious.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.638
The first print statement should print zero,&nbsp;
 that's the default class variable.

00:00:45.638 --> 00:00:48.578
And the second should print 42.

00:00:48.578 --> 00:00:54.120
But when we actually&nbsp;run the 
 code, we see zero printed both times.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.375
But there's what, 11 lines of code here.

00:00:56.375 --> 00:00:59.040
What could I possibly be doing wrong?

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.234
If you don't already understand what's going&nbsp;on,

00:01:01.234 --> 00:01:03.376
the frustration of a bug like this,

00:01:03.376 --> 00:01:06.106
THIS IS OUTRAGEOUS, IT'S UNFAIR!

00:01:06.106 --> 00:01:09.519
HOW CAN YOU BE ON THE COUNCIL 
 AND NOT BE A MASTER?

00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:14.506
No, I'm not using a non-ASCII look-alike 
 character&nbsp;or some other thing to trick your eyes.

00:01:14.506 --> 00:01:19.012
But yes, it does have something 
 to do&nbsp;with the name of the variable.

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:23.820
In particular, it has everything to&nbsp;
 do with these two leading underscores.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.827
If you just name the 
 variable 'count' instead&nbsp;of '__count',

00:01:27.827 --> 00:01:31.722
you would get the output that 
 you're probably expecting in the first place.

00:01:31.722 --> 00:01:35.340
So&nbsp;something funny is 
 going on with the underscores.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.099
But if I use the setter, everything 
 would have&nbsp;worked fine.

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:43.634
It was only when I tried to 
 directly change the object that it didn't work.

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:48.240
And to&nbsp;add to the 
 confusion, the value 42 is in there.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.587
When I directly set the value of the count,

00:01:50.587 --> 00:01:53.411
and I print it out by directly grabbing its value,

00:01:53.411 --> 00:01:55.730
we do see the 42.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:02:00.300
It's just the&nbsp;call to 'get_count' that still returns 0.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.614
To figure out what's going on

00:02:01.614 --> 00:02:06.786
let's print&nbsp;the dictionary of this 
 object after setting the count with both methods.

00:02:06.786 --> 00:02:09.661
Very surprisingly, if&nbsp;you're not aware of this feature,

00:02:09.661 --> 00:02:13.831
the dictionary contains two different count variables.

00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:16.860
One&nbsp;of them is the one that we intended to set.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.216
And the setter apparently set this variable.

00:02:20.216 --> 00:02:21.131
W H A T ?

00:02:21.131 --> 00:02:24.224
And likewise, the getter 
 appears to be getting that weird variable,

00:02:24.224 --> 00:02:27.180
not&nbsp;the variable that we set in our code.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.877
Well, let me introduce you to the questionable&nbsp;
 feature behind all of this confusion:

00:02:31.877 --> 00:02:34.591
Private Name Mangling

00:02:34.591 --> 00:02:39.263
Basically, if you're inside a class and&nbsp;
 you name a variable with two leading underscores,

00:02:39.263 --> 00:02:46.140
then Python will replace all uses of that&nbsp;
 variable with a mangled version of that variable.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.825
The reason for doing this is 
 to sort of&nbsp;support private access specifiers

00:02:49.825 --> 00:02:53.940
like they have in other languages like C++ and Java.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.143
Suppose I'm building a GUI 
 library, and I have&nbsp;a base widget class.

00:02:58.143 --> 00:03:02.450
I keep count of all the widgets and 
 increase it whenever I create a new&nbsp;one.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.990
I also use the count to give each widget a unique ID.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:11.760
But there are going to be tons of&nbsp;
 different widgets, text boxes, labels, buttons.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.680
But in some subclass, like this 
 button,&nbsp;I might also have some kind of count.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.273
In this case, the button's count 
 means something&nbsp;totally different.

00:03:20.273 --> 00:03:23.385
The button is counting 
 how many times it's been clicked.

00:03:23.385 --> 00:03:28.910
Whereas, the count&nbsp;in widget was like the total 
 number of widgets that had been created before this button.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:32.340
But&nbsp;maybe, I have a whole class hierarchy of widgets,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.843
And I didn't write all of them.

00:03:34.843 --> 00:03:38.873
Most of them were&nbsp;written by a 
 mix of ten different open-source developers.

00:03:38.873 --> 00:03:43.320
And of course, anyone that uses 
 the&nbsp;library is free to create their own widgets.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:49.380
Because of this automatic private 
 name mangling&nbsp;that happens, there's no conflict here.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:55.020
All uses of this '__count' are automatically&nbsp;
 replaced with this '_widget__count'.&nbsp;

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.820
Whereas, all usages of this '__count'
 get replaced with '_Button_count'.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.702
Because of the private name mangling,

00:04:01.702 --> 00:04:05.244
the&nbsp;author of the 'Button' 
 class doesn't need to know or care

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:09.660
about the variables and private&nbsp;
 implementation details of the widget class.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.020
Everything just works, and there's no conflict.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.980
At least, that's how it's&nbsp;supposed to work in theory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.601
But in reality, this picture 
 comes with a&nbsp;lot of asterisks.

00:04:21.601 --> 00:04:27.342
The first very important asterisk here 
 is that Python does not have private&nbsp;variables.

00:04:27.342 --> 00:04:30.720
All variables in Python are public.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.199
This private name mangling 
 just changes the name&nbsp;of the variable.

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:38.277
It doesn't actually prevent anyone from accessing it.

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:40.036
So if you know how&nbsp;name mangling works,

00:04:40.036 --> 00:04:44.475
you can still reach inside the 
 class and change anything that you like.

00:04:44.475 --> 00:04:49.725
The intention of name mangling 
 is to prevent accidental reusing of variables,

00:04:49.725 --> 00:04:54.720
not purposeful&nbsp;modifications by 
 a nefarious class author.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.252
The second asterisk is that 
 the rules for how name&nbsp;mangling works

00:04:58.252 --> 00:05:03.064
and which variables it applies to are pretty weird.

00:05:03.064 --> 00:05:07.800
Python does this name mangling&nbsp;
 if your variable starts with two underscores.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.720
Unless it also ends with two&nbsp;
 underscores, then it's not mangled.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.987
Including if your variable is 
 just two or three&nbsp;underscores.

00:05:16.987 --> 00:05:21.084
And it does this for all 
 identifiers, including local variables.

00:05:21.084 --> 00:05:26.311
So if you were to&nbsp;look at the local 
 variables of this function, you would see this.

00:05:26.311 --> 00:05:29.558
Including, for some reason,&nbsp;imports,

00:05:29.558 --> 00:05:33.540
unless the import has a dot in the name.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.711
And it doesn't just do 
 this on 'self' and local&nbsp;variables.

00:05:35.711 --> 00:05:40.108
It does it on any usage 
 of a variable name of this form.

00:05:40.108 --> 00:05:44.554
And the mangling is done based&nbsp;
 on the class where the code is defined in.

00:05:44.554 --> 00:05:48.067
Not the class of the object.

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:52.800
So this statement will&nbsp;
 try to set this '_a__version' of the x variable.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.497
Even if b isn't an instance of a.

00:05:56.497 --> 00:06:01.020
Because this&nbsp;init function 
 was defined inside of the A class.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.030
It's going to use A here.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.780
But it&nbsp;doesn't just use the class name.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.713
If you add underscores to the beginning 
 of&nbsp;the class, that doesn't change the mangling.

00:06:10.713 --> 00:06:15.060
So, even if you have a bunch of underscores&nbsp;
 at the beginning or, more realistically,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:19.991
one underscore at the beginning, the&nbsp;
mangled name will still just have one&nbsp;underscore.

00:06:19.991 --> 00:06:23.325
Unless the class name is only&nbsp;underscores.

00:06:23.325 --> 00:06:25.609
Then nothing is ever mangled.

00:06:25.609 --> 00:06:26.109
.

00:06:26.109 --> 00:06:26.609
. .

00:06:26.609 --> 00:06:27.109
. . .

00:06:27.109 --> 00:06:27.609
. . . .

00:06:27.609 --> 00:06:28.109
. . . . .

00:06:28.109 --> 00:06:28.609
. . . . . .

00:06:28.609 --> 00:06:29.109
. . . . . . .

00:06:29.109 --> 00:06:29.609
. . . . . . . . 

00:06:29.609 --> 00:06:30.109
. . . . . . . . .

00:06:30.109 --> 00:06:30.609
. . . . . . . . . .

00:06:30.609 --> 00:06:31.109
. . . . . . . . . . . . .

00:06:31.109 --> 00:06:31.609
. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .

00:06:31.609 --> 00:06:32.109
. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .

00:06:32.109 --> 00:06:32.609
. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .

00:06:32.609 --> 00:06:33.300
