00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.661
If you're interested, here's a Python 
 implementation of how the mangling would&nbsp;actually work

00:06:37.661 --> 00:06:41.760
based on the given class name and identifier.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.359
And if you're looking for the actual&nbsp;
 CPython implementation,

00:06:44.359 --> 00:06:47.439
then you're looking for this 'Py_Mangle' function.

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:50.400
Don't worry. I'm&nbsp;not 
 actually going to go over the C code.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.047
But the one thing that I do want to take 
 away from&nbsp;this is actually the name of the file this is in.

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:58.141
It's called 'compile.c'.

00:06:58.141 --> 00:07:00.346
That's right. As&nbsp;if it wasn't already confusing enough,

00:07:00.346 --> 00:07:03.293
name mangling actually happens at compile time.

00:07:03.293 --> 00:07:06.733
Yes,&nbsp;Python is compiled and it is also interpreted.

00:07:06.733 --> 00:07:09.647
And the name mangling happens at compile time.

00:07:09.647 --> 00:07:13.186
So that means if I have 
 this 'get_count' example like at the beginning,

00:07:13.186 --> 00:07:17.774
the usage of '__count'&nbsp;here isn't 
 mangled because it's not inside the class definition.

00:07:17.774 --> 00:07:20.880
And we see 0,&nbsp;just 
 like at the beginning of the video.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.020
But if I had defined this 
 'get_count' function&nbsp;outside of the class

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.304
and then assigned it at runtime,

00:07:27.304 --> 00:07:31.555
then because when this function&nbsp;
 was compiled, it was not inside of a class,

00:07:31.555 --> 00:07:34.642
this usage of 'self.__count' doesn't get mangled.

00:07:34.642 --> 00:07:38.160
And therefore we see 21 instead of 0.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.530
So literally just moving where the function was&nbsp;
 defined can change the behavior.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:46.437
That means in any class 
 depending on this private name mangling,

00:07:46.437 --> 00:07:49.800
you&nbsp;can't use many 
 of the dynamic features of Python,

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.000
No dynamically created 
 methods, metaclasses, or&nbsp;decorator magic.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.000
That's not to say those things can't work.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:02.040
But it just becomes incredibly,&nbsp;
 incredibly confusing in order to make it work.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.141
If you want to stay safe and not worry about it,

00:08:04.141 --> 00:08:07.910
you're pretty much 
 limited to features of static languages.

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:09.359
Which is not completely bad.

00:08:09.359 --> 00:08:12.659
In fact,&nbsp;I recommend 
 that, especially for larger projects,

00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:17.235
you self-impose a lot of the 
 restrictions&nbsp;that static languages force on you.

00:08:17.235 --> 00:08:19.560
But that's besides the point. Just be aware.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.357
If you're using&nbsp;mangled names,

00:08:21.357 --> 00:08:25.396
there is a very high chance that 
 other developers, including yourself in the&nbsp;future,

00:08:25.396 --> 00:08:28.800
will be confused by what's going on.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.142
In order to get a feel for when 
 and how Python&nbsp;does this mangling

00:08:32.142 --> 00:08:36.862
and to verify that it actually does 
 happen at compile time, not at runtime,

00:08:36.862 --> 00:08:40.320
I&nbsp;highly recommend you check out Compiler Explorer.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.740
For Python, It shows you the&nbsp;
 bytecode output of your module.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.580
You don't need to understand all the bytecode&nbsp;
 in order to see what's going on here.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:52.260
Put in a recognizable constant so you can&nbsp;
 find where that corresponds to in the bytecode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.621
Very nearby. Notice we have this "store_attr".

00:08:55.621 --> 00:08:58.723
And&nbsp;there we see it, a mangled name.

00:08:58.723 --> 00:09:01.399
You can see how if we don't 
 meet the requirements of a mangled&nbsp;name,

00:09:01.399 --> 00:09:05.805
like just having a single 
 underscore, then the mangling doesn't happen.

00:09:05.805 --> 00:09:10.500
Same thing if&nbsp;we had two 
 trailing underscores. No mangling.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:16.380
We can use this to see that the mangling 
 still&nbsp;happens even if we're just using a local variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:21.180
And we can see that it still happens 
 even&nbsp;if we have a deeply nested dotted name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:26.700
Any use of this identifier will get&nbsp;
 mangled as long as we're inside the class.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:30.511
But if we're not inside a 
 class, look&nbsp;for that constant here.

00:09:30.511 --> 00:09:33.419
Then the name doesn't get mangled.

00:09:33.419 --> 00:09:34.503
Please try this yourself.

00:09:34.503 --> 00:09:39.296
Check out all the different cases 
 of variable names and class names and whatever.

00:09:39.296 --> 00:09:43.200
And see for&nbsp;yourself when 
 and where the mangling happens.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.580
My favorite absolutely&nbsp;cursed case of this is this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.284
Of course, I've literally never seen an 
 actual&nbsp;module that's prefixed with two underscores.

00:09:51.284 --> 00:09:55.258
But just so you can see what the compiler does.

00:09:55.258 --> 00:09:58.860
We can&nbsp;see Python does 
 not mangle the name of the module,

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:02.926
But it does store the 
 module under a mangled name.

00:10:02.926 --> 00:10:06.960
But if we just import a double underscore module,

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.871
then both the stored name 
 and the imported&nbsp;name are mangled.

00:10:11.871 --> 00:10:14.764
And I guess it's mostly just curiosity at this point.

00:10:14.764 --> 00:10:17.700
Even&nbsp;I wouldn't do something this cursed.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:21.096
Now that we understand that 
 name mangling happens&nbsp;at compile time

00:10:21.096 --> 00:10:24.415
and after a brief break to calm down,

00:10:24.415 --> 00:10:27.618
I just wanted to address 
 what's usually the&nbsp;most common question.

00:10:27.618 --> 00:10:32.153
And that's how does name 
 mangling work with 'getattr' and 'setattr'?

00:10:32.153 --> 00:10:34.391
Mangled names are just names at runtime.

00:10:34.391 --> 00:10:36.422
So if you want to use 'getattr' and 'setattr',

00:10:36.422 --> 00:10:39.780
you&nbsp;need to just manually mangle the name yourself.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:42.966
And additionally, mangling 
 does not apply&nbsp;inside strings.

00:10:42.966 --> 00:10:45.585
It only applies to identifiers.

00:10:45.585 --> 00:10:49.107
So think variable names and dotted names.

00:10:49.107 --> 00:10:51.964
If&nbsp;you use 'getattr' or 
 'setattr' and forget to mangle the name,

00:10:51.964 --> 00:10:55.260
you're probably going to&nbsp;
 get an error or set the wrong attribute.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.628
So finally, we need to 
 address the elephant in the&nbsp;room.

00:10:58.628 --> 00:11:02.541
And that is, because of the 
 confusion and complexity of name mangling,

00:11:02.541 --> 00:11:06.772
do people actually&nbsp;
 use private name mangling in practice?

00:11:06.772 --> 00:11:11.358
And should you use 
 private name mangling in practice?

00:11:11.358 --> 00:11:13.433
In my&nbsp;actual experience programming in Python,

00:11:13.433 --> 00:11:16.242
I pretty much never use this.

00:11:16.242 --> 00:11:20.766
And I pretty much never&nbsp;
 see it in anyone else's code either.

00:11:20.766 --> 00:11:23.387
So what do I see?

00:11:23.387 --> 00:11:28.051
The most common thing that 
 I see is single&nbsp;underscore variable names.

00:11:28.051 --> 00:11:31.290
Now, different people will tell 
 you that a single underscore prefix&nbsp;means that

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:34.503
a variable should be treated as a private variable

00:11:34.503 --> 00:11:38.160
or a protected variable or an&nbsp;implementation detail.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.318
And I'm not going to argue about the 
 technical definition of what it should&nbsp;be called.

00:11:42.318 --> 00:11:46.138
Suffice it to say that the convention 
 and the popular unspoken rule is that

00:11:46.138 --> 00:11:50.280
an&nbsp;underscore prefix variable means "don't touch me."

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.222
If the author of the class 
 meant&nbsp;for you to be able to change it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.222 --> 00:11:54.994
they would give you a way to do that.

00:11:54.994 --> 00:11:57.180
Often through getters and setters.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:02.003
Rarely, I do see this dunder 
 form of an&nbsp;attribute to mean private.

00:12:02.003 --> 00:12:07.065
But actually, I more often see 
 that as a public kind of magic&nbsp;attribute.

00:12:07.065 --> 00:12:10.320
More like a dunder-len or a dunder-str.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.649
These are typically not private.

00:12:11.649 --> 00:12:16.440
It's very common&nbsp;and 
 expected for a class to override these.

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:21.600
But the problem is that these approaches 
 don't&nbsp;actually solve the original issue that we had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.284
Remember, we had a hierarchy of widgets.

00:12:24.284 --> 00:12:28.296
And we&nbsp;didn't want someone writing a widget 
 to have to understand the implementation details.

00:12:28.296 --> 00:12:32.220
And avoid&nbsp;the variable 
 names of all the parent classes.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:35.725
If I just use single 
 underscore prefixes on my&nbsp;variables,

00:12:35.725 --> 00:12:37.819
that's certainly clear to readers of my code that:

00:12:37.819 --> 00:12:41.280
This is an implementation&nbsp;
 detail. You're not supposed to touch.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.798
But in order to know what all 
 those&nbsp;attributes are that I shouldn't touch,

00:12:44.798 --> 00:12:48.420
I need to end up reading all the&nbsp;
 source code of all my parent classes.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:52.920
Otherwise, how do I know that my  parent
 class&nbsp;doesn't have their own 'don't-touch-me' variable?

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.291
And so you have a choice, a trade-off.

00:12:56.291 --> 00:12:57.596
You&nbsp;could use a single underscore.

00:12:57.596 --> 00:13:02.888
And force that every class that inherits from you 
 needs to know&nbsp;the implementation details of your class.

00:13:02.888 --> 00:13:05.181
Or you could use private name mangling.

00:13:05.181 --> 00:13:07.190
That way people&nbsp;that want to subclass your class

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:10.284
don't have to worry about 
 any of your implementation details.

00:13:10.284 --> 00:13:13.794
But you pay the price in 
 terms of using this confusing feature.

00:13:13.794 --> 00:13:17.940
And you lose some of that&nbsp;
 ability to use Python's dynamic capabilities.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:21.352
Or, and this is also a very questionable&nbsp;idea,

00:13:21.352 --> 00:13:24.120
you could manually mangle the names.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:26.867
Subclasses don't have to 
 know about your&nbsp;implementation.

00:13:26.867 --> 00:13:30.235
There's no unexpected, hidden, or confusing side effects.

00:13:30.235 --> 00:13:33.552
But your code&nbsp;looks 
 horrible, and your co-workers hate you.

00:13:33.552 --> 00:13:36.840
Honestly, it's a trade-off 
 you&nbsp;need to decide for yourself.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:41.740
But full disclosure, in the wild, the&nbsp;most 
 common approach is to use the&nbsp;single underscore.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:44.724
And to just hope subclasses&nbsp;
 don't clobber your implementation details.

00:13:44.724 --> 00:13:48.648
If you're very good about keeping your 
 classes&nbsp;defined as if they were statically typed,

00:13:48.648 --> 00:13:50.955
then you could also write a letter rule to help you.

00:13:50.955 --> 00:13:54.120
But in any case, go forth and decide for yourself.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.065
As always, thank you for 
 watching and making&nbsp;it to the end.

00:13:57.065 --> 00:13:59.323
If you enjoyed my video, please do subscribe.

00:13:59.323 --> 00:14:02.855
And if you especially enjoyed&nbsp;it, 
 please consider becoming a patron or donor.

